title: The verb pattern
tags: [[Command line]]
kind: rationale
summary: get and set, add and remove, add and delete: the same verbs, the same meaning, at every level — and in the daemon and the GUI too.

The data commands form a tree (`metarecord`, `field`) whose verbs follow one
pattern at every level:

* `get` reads; `set` replaces ''everything at that level'';
* `add` creates a new element, with a new id or uuid;
* `remove` takes one out ''by its value'';
* `delete` destroys one ''addressed by its id'' (or uuid).

So `get` is the inverse of `set`, and `add` the inverse of `remove` (by value) and
of `delete` (by id). The two removals are not synonyms: `remove` names a value
inside a multi-valued field and leaves its other rows alone, `delete` destroys a
thing that has an id — a metarecord (`mf metarecord delete`), a field row
(`mf field delete <id>`). Keeping them apart is what stops "take this tag off the
record" from reading like "destroy the record".

`set` on a metarecord replaces all its fields; on a field name, all the rows of
that name; on a row, the one row. `add` exists only where new ids are born — a
metarecord, a row under a field name — so the by-id `field` group has none.

The same four verbs name the daemon's set layer (`POST /query/fields/{set,add,
remove,unset}`) and the GUI's `metarecord:field` and `metarecord:bulk` operations:
one word means one thing in all three.
